//! lotus/stereo_compress_env.rye -- ALES161: the ATTACK/RELEASE COMPRESSOR carried into stereo -- the LINKED envelope (ALES160) driving ONE LINKED gain (ALES157) over time. ALES157's stereo_compress leans on a pair the
//! instant it sees it: a peak the moment it arrives is already softened, and the softening vanishes the moment the peak passes. A real compressor breathes -- it hears a peak ARRIVE and leans in over a few samples (the
//! attack), then eases the gain back as the sound falls away (the release). ALES160 built exactly that breath for stereo (one shared envelope tracking the linked peak) and proved it once. This rung SPENDS both proofs: it
//! drives the gain reduction from the smoothed LINKED envelope rather than the bare pair, and applies the ONE resulting gain to both channels -- a compressor that leans in patiently and lets go gently, the image held to the
//! sample the whole time.
//!
//! Two proofs fused. The DETECTOR is linked and time-smoothed (ALES160): the one envelope steps toward max(|left|, |right|) clamped to the rail, the louder channel deciding the shared loudness over time. The GAIN is linked
//! (ALES157): where the smoothed envelope crosses the threshold the excess is softened by the ratio (ALES50's ceiling, comp = threshold + excess-den/num, computed on the ENVELOPE not the bare sample) and the single gain
//! comp/e is applied to BOTH raw samples. Compressing each channel by its own envelope would smooth and soften on different samples and pump the image; one detector and one gain keep the field aligned.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadThreshold (outside [1, sample_max]), BadRatio (below one or a zero denominator), BadCoeff (an attack or release outside (0, 1]), and BadRange (a span past the shared length) BEFORE any
//! write. Otherwise walk the span one sample-PAIR at a time, advancing ONE i64 envelope toward the linked peak by ALES160's stereo_env_step: envelope within the threshold leaves the pair playing (both pass byte for byte);
//! envelope above the threshold applies the one softened gain comp/e to both raw samples.
//!
//! The crux -- six laws. THE INSTANTANEOUS LAW (the crux link): unit attack and unit release make the linked envelope the instantaneous linked peak, so this equals ALES157's memoryless stereo_compress byte for byte on both --
//! the time-varying rung's zero-smoothing limit is exactly the memoryless rung already proven. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW: a left-always-peak master has left' equal to ALES53's mono compress_env(left) byte for byte, the
//! right scaled by the same fraction comp/e, the independent per-channel compress_env genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase pair returning still out of phase, no
//! magnitude expanding, no sign flipping. THE KNOB LAWS: unit ratio the identity everywhere, a slower attack letting more of a transient through (monotone). THE CARRY LAW: a span compressed in two pieces equals the whole
//! once on both. THE SUB-THRESHOLD / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a master whose linked envelope never crosses the threshold passing byte for byte, silence staying silence, left.len == right.len after,
//! each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES161.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-065200_fill-ales161-lotus-stereo-compress-env.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_envelope.rye (ALES160) -- the LINKED envelope, its stereo_env_step advancing the one shared detector toward the linked peak, reused whole; lotus/stereo_compress.rye (ALES157) -- the LINKED gain this
//! drives over time, its instantaneous form the unit-coefficient limit re-proven beside it; lotus/compress_env.rye (ALES53) -- the mono attack/release compressor, its CompressEnvError reused whole and its output the
//! left-always-peak comparison; lotus/compress.rye (ALES50) -- the softened ceiling comp = threshold + excess-den/num; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, sample_max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) carrying one shared i64 envelope, siloed to lotus/. One linked-peak comparison and at most one multiply-then-divide for the
//! envelope step plus one per channel for the gain, all in i64 (|sample|-comp <= 32768-32767 never overflowing), the gain comp/e in (0, 1] so every scaled magnitude is at most its input's and fits i16. The threshold is a
//! magnitude in sample units, the ratio a plain fraction, the attack and release fractions per sample index rather than milliseconds against a clock. No real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no
//! real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_compress_env.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const envelope = @import("envelope.rye");
const stereo_envelope = @import("stereo_envelope.rye");
const stereo_compress = @import("stereo_compress.rye");
const compress_env = @import("compress_env.rye");
const compress = @import("compress.rye");

pub const stereo_compress_env_version = "20260815.065200";

/// The linked attack/release compressor's faults are exactly ALES53's own `CompressEnvError` -- `BadThreshold`, `BadRatio`, `BadCoeff`, `BadRange` -- reused by name, since fusing the linked detector with the linked gain adds
/// no new fault: it validates one shared threshold, one shared ratio, one shared attack, one shared release, and one span, then advances a single envelope and applies one gain per pair from both channels together.
pub const StereoCompressEnvError = compress_env.CompressEnvError;

/// Compress `count` sample-PAIRS starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc` above the magnitude `threshold` by the ratio `ratio_num/ratio_den`, the ONE gain reduction FOLLOWING the LINKED envelope that CONTINUES from the
/// caller's shared `env` (carried-state, ALES43's lesson). Each pair advances ALES160's linked follower one step toward the linked peak `max(|left|, |right|)` (the attack fraction while rising, release while falling); where
/// the smoothed envelope sits within the threshold both channels pass byte for byte, and where it rises above, the envelope's excess is softened by the ratio (`comp = threshold + excess-den/num`, ALES50's ceiling on the
/// linked envelope) and the single gain `comp/e` applied to BOTH raw samples -- one detector, one gain, the image held. The caller owns the i64 envelope across calls, so a span compressed in two pieces equals the whole once.
/// Refuses BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff, and BadRange before any write; on refusal `env` and both channels are untouched. Values only -- never the length, and the channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_compress_follow_carry(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    env: *i64,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
) StereoCompressEnvError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared gain preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the threshold is a legal magnitude -- at least one sample unit and no greater than the i16 rail. Before any write.
    if (threshold < 1) return error.BadThreshold;
    if (threshold > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadThreshold;
    // invariant: the ratio is at least one (num >= den) with a nonzero denominator -- a ratio below one would expand (an upward gesture), a zero denominator would divide by zero. A compressor only ever leans down.
    if (ratio_den == 0) return error.BadRatio;
    if (ratio_num < ratio_den) return error.BadRatio;
    // invariant: both attack and release coefficients are legal fractions in (0, 1] -- checked by the follower's one proven rule.
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(attack_num, attack_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(release_num, release_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the SHARED length -- checked once, without underflow, so it holds identically for both channels and a refusal never compresses one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const thr: i64 = @as(i64, threshold);
    const num: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_num);
    const den: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_den);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) {
        const xl: i16 = sc.left.buf[start + i];
        const xr: i16 = sc.right.buf[start + i];
        // Advance the ONE shared linked follower toward the pair's linked peak (ALES160's proven step); e is the smoothed linked magnitude, in [0, sample_max].
        _ = stereo_envelope.stereo_env_step(env, xl, xr, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den);
        const e: i64 = env.*;
        // invariant: the linked envelope stays a legal magnitude -- never negative, never past the rail (the follower's own guarantee).
        assert(e >= 0 and e <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        if (e > thr) {
            const excess: i64 = e - thr; // strictly positive: the smoothed linked detector has crossed the ceiling
            // The softened envelope target -- ALES50's ceiling computed on the LINKED envelope, not the bare sample.
            const comp: i64 = thr + @divTrunc(excess * den, num);
            // invariant: the softened envelope sits within [threshold, envelope] -- the single gain comp/e is in (0, 1].
            assert(comp >= thr and comp <= e);
            const ml: i64 = if (xl < 0) -@as(i64, xl) else @as(i64, xl); // |left| — safe in i64 (|sample_min| = 32768 fits)
            const mr: i64 = if (xr < 0) -@as(i64, xr) else @as(i64, xr); // |right|
            // ONE shared gain comp/e across both channels: m-comp <= 32768-32767 fits i64; divTrunc keeps each quotient at most its m.
            const nl: i64 = @divTrunc(ml * comp, e);
            const nr: i64 = @divTrunc(mr * comp, e);
            // invariant: the shared gain reduces, never boosts, and never below zero, on each channel -- a smoothed compressor only ever leans down.
            assert(nl <= ml and nl >= 0 and nr <= mr and nr >= 0);
            const yl: i16 = @intCast(if (xl < 0) -nl else nl); // safe: 0 <= nl <= ml <= sample_max
            const yr: i16 = @intCast(if (xr < 0) -nr else nr);
            sc.left.buf[start + i] = yl;
            sc.right.buf[start + i] = yr;
            // invariant: each written sample holds its input's sign (magnitude only shrank), zero being sign-neutral.
            assert((xl >= 0 and yl >= 0) or (xl <= 0 and yl <= 0));
            assert((xr >= 0 and yr >= 0) or (xr <= 0 and yr <= 0));
        }
        // envelope within the threshold: the pair plays -- both left exactly as they were (the gain reduction has not opened).
    }

    // invariant: a compressor changes sample values only -- each channel holds its starting length and the channels leave balanced, the stereo image aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

/// Compress `count` sample-pairs starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc`, the one gain reduction following a LINKED envelope that begins FROM SILENCE (a fresh shared env of 0) -- one implementation shared with the
/// carried form, so the two cannot drift. Refuses BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff, and BadRange before any write.
pub fn stereo_compress_follow(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
) StereoCompressEnvError!void {
    var env: i64 = 0;
    return stereo_compress_follow_carry(sc, start, count, &env, threshold, ratio_num, ratio_den, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den);
}

// --- selftest: THE INSTANTANEOUS LAW -- unit attack and release reduce it to ALES157's memoryless stereo_compress byte for byte on both; THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW -- a left-always-peak master has left' == ALES53's mono
//     compress_env(left), the right scaled by the same fraction, the independent lift differing; THE IMAGE LAW -- no magnitude expands, no sign flips, an out-of-phase pair returns still out of phase; THE KNOB LAWS -- unit
//     ratio the identity, a slower attack letting the transient through; THE CARRY LAW -- a span in two pieces equals the whole once on both; THE SUB-THRESHOLD / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW ------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE INSTANTANEOUS LAW -- the crux link. With unit attack AND unit release the linked envelope IS the instantaneous linked peak (ALES160's unit identity), so the gain application lands each pair at exactly ALES157's
    //    memoryless stereo_compress output. Proven by running BOTH tools on the same master and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 5000, -5000, 800, -9000, 9000, -200, 6000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 3000, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -12000, 12000 };
        var smoothed = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&smoothed, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_compress_follow(&smoothed, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1); // instant attack, instant release
        var plain = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&plain, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_compress.stereo_compress(&plain, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1); // the proven ALES157 memoryless linked compressor
        if (!eq(smoothed.left.samples(), plain.left.samples())) return error.UnitLeftNotMemoryless;
        if (!eq(smoothed.right.samples(), plain.right.samples())) return error.UnitRightNotMemoryless;
    }

    // 2. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW -- the stereo crux. A master where the LEFT always holds the peak (|left| >= |right| everywhere) makes the shared envelope equal the mono envelope of the left, so left' equals ALES53's
    //    mono compress_env(left) byte for byte, and the right is scaled by the SAME fraction comp/e -- a different, image-preserving result than compressing the right on its own.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 2000, -14000, 800, -9000, 12000, -300, 6000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 6000, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -3000, 3000 }; // |right| <= |left| everywhere
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_compress_follow(&sc, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5);

        // the peak-holding left equals ALES53's mono compress_env of it, proven against the mono tool directly.
        var mono_l = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_l, 0, &ll);
        try compress_env.compress_follow(&mono_l, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), mono_l.samples())) return error.PeakLeftNotMono;

        // the INDEPENDENT lift genuinely differs -- mono compress_env of the right alone follows the right's own (quieter) envelope, an image-shifting result.
        var mono_r = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_r, 0, &rr);
        try compress_env.compress_follow(&mono_r, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5);
        if (eq(sc.right.samples(), mono_r.samples())) return error.IndependentDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 3. THE IMAGE LAW -- the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio. Over a mixed master no output magnitude exceeds its input's and no sign flips; and an out-of-phase pair (right = −left) comes back STILL out of phase, both
    //    scaled by the identical time-smoothed fraction.
    {
        const il = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 15000, -400, 9000, -9000 };
        const ir = [_]i16{ -400, 600, -3000, 200, -700, 700 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &il, &ir);
        try stereo_compress_follow(&sc, 0, 6, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 2, 1, 6);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 6) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(l[i]) > abs16(il[i]) or abs16(r[i]) > abs16(ir[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded;
            if ((il[i] > 0 and l[i] < 0) or (il[i] < 0 and l[i] > 0)) return error.LeftSignFlipped;
            if ((ir[i] > 0 and r[i] < 0) or (ir[i] < 0 and r[i] > 0)) return error.RightSignFlipped;
        }

        var op = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&op, &[_]i16{ 6000, -8000, 12000, -4000 }, &[_]i16{ -6000, 8000, -12000, 4000 });
        try stereo_compress_follow(&op, 0, 4, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 2, 1, 6);
        const ol = op.left.samples();
        const orr = op.right.samples();
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < 4) : (j += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, orr[j]) != -@as(i32, ol[j])) return error.OutOfPhaseNotPreserved;
        }
    }

    // 4a. UNIT RATIO IS THE IDENTITY EVERYWHERE on both -- ratio_num == ratio_den makes comp == e, so the gain is exactly one at every sample regardless of smoothing: the whole master passes byte for byte.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 500, -20000, 20000, -400 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 600, -700, 2000, -50, 100 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_compress_follow(&sc, 0, 6, 1000, 3, 3, 1, 4, 1, 8); // unit ratio 3:3, real attack/release
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.UnitRatioNotIdentity;
    }

    // 4b. A SLOWER ATTACK LETS THE TRANSIENT THROUGH -- on a sudden loud onset after quiet, a slower attack reduces the first above-threshold pairs LESS than an instant attack (the gain reduction lags the linked peak). So the
    //     slow-attack magnitude is no smaller than the instant-attack magnitude at every sample on the left, and the first onset sample is strictly louder under the slow attack (the transient audibly survives).
    {
        const ol = [_]i16{ 100, 100, 15000, 15000, 15000, 15000, 15000, 15000 }; // quiet, then a hard onset on the left (it holds the linked peak)
        const orr = [_]i16{ -50, 50, -3000, 3000, -3000, 3000, -3000, 3000 };
        const thr: i32 = 1000;
        var slow = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&slow, &ol, &orr);
        try stereo_compress_follow(&slow, 0, 8, thr, 8, 1, 1, 8, 1, 8); // slow attack 1:8
        var fast = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&fast, &ol, &orr);
        try stereo_compress_follow(&fast, 0, 8, thr, 8, 1, 1, 1, 1, 8); // instant attack 1:1
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 8) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(slow.left.samples()[i]) < abs16(fast.left.samples()[i])) return error.SlowAttackClampedMore;
        }
        if (abs16(slow.left.samples()[2]) <= abs16(fast.left.samples()[2])) return error.TransientDidNotSurvive;
    }

    // 5. THE CARRY LAW -- compressing a span in two pieces (the second continuing the first's ending shared envelope) equals compressing the whole span once, byte for byte on BOTH channels.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 2000, -14000, 14000, -2000, 9000, -9000, 6000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 6000, -1000, 2000, -700, 800, -3000, 3000 };
        var whole = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&whole, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_compress_follow(&whole, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5);

        var split = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&split, &ll, &rr);
        var env: i64 = 0;
        try stereo_compress_follow_carry(&split, 0, 3, &env, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5); // first piece
        try stereo_compress_follow_carry(&split, 3, 5, &env, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5); // second continues the shared state
        if (!eq(split.left.samples(), whole.left.samples())) return error.CarryLeftNotEqual;
        if (!eq(split.right.samples(), whole.right.samples())) return error.CarryRightNotEqual;
    }

    // 6a. THE SUB-THRESHOLD LAW -- a master whose linked envelope never crosses the threshold passes byte for byte on both (the gain reduction never opens).
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 300, -800, 200, -900, 500 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -50, 400, -700, 80, -100 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_compress_follow(&sc, 0, 5, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8); // threshold 1000 above every magnitude, envelope never crosses
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.SubThresholdNotIdentity;
    }

    // 6b. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- an all-silent master compresses to zero on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_compress_follow(&sc, 0, 4, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 500, -800, 200, -900, 100 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 300, -700, 500, -50 });
        try stereo_compress_follow(&bc, 0, 5, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 6c. THE ATOMICITY LAW -- a bad threshold, a sub-unity ratio, a zero denominator, an illegal coefficient, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_compress_follow(&a, 0, 3, 0, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8)) |_| return error.ZeroThresholdSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongZeroThreshold;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.ThresholdRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_compress_follow(&b, 0, 3, 1000, 1, 2, 1, 4, 1, 8)) |_| return error.SubUnitySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRatio) return error.WrongSubUnity;
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.SubUnityRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_compress_follow(&c, 0, 3, 1000, 4, 1, 5, 4, 1, 8)) |_| return error.BadAttackSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadAttack;
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.AttackRefusalMutated;

        var d = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&d, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_compress_follow(&d, 1, 3, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(d.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(d.right.samples(), &tr) or d.left.len != d.right.len) return error.BadRangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 6d. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no pair to compress.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 5000, -5000, 9000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_compress_follow(&noop, 1, 0, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5000, -5000, 9000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    // silence the unused import in builds that trim it -- compress is named in the doc lineage; touch it so the reference stays honest.
    _ = compress.compress_version;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-compress-env: the ATTACK/RELEASE COMPRESSOR CARRIED INTO STEREO — the LINKED envelope driving ONE LINKED gain over time. Compress a span of sample-PAIRS in a master above a magnitude threshold " ++
        "by a ratio, the one gain reduction following the shared linked envelope (max(|left|, |right|), attack while rising, release while falling): within the threshold both pass byte for byte, above it the softened target " ++
        "comp = threshold + excess·den/num (ALES50's ceiling on the linked envelope) yields one gain comp/e applied to both raw samples. ALES157's memoryless stereo_compress leans on a pair the instant it sees it; ALES160 " ++
        "built the linked breath; this fuses them — one detector, one gain, the image held. THE INSTANTANEOUS LAW (the crux link): unit attack and release make the linked envelope the instantaneous linked peak, so this " ++
        "equals ALES157's memoryless stereo_compress byte for byte on both. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW: a left-always-peak master has left' == ALES53's mono compress_env(left), the right scaled by the same fraction, the " ++
        "independent per-channel compress_env genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase pair returns still out of phase, no magnitude expands, no sign flips. THE KNOB LAWS: " ++
        "unit ratio the identity everywhere, a slower attack letting more of a transient through (monotone). THE CARRY LAW: a span compressed in two pieces equals the whole once on both. THE SUB-THRESHOLD / SILENCE / " ++
        "BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a master whose linked envelope never crosses the threshold passing byte for byte, silence staying silence, left.len == right.len after, each of the four refusals leaving both " ++
        "channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips carrying one shared i64 envelope, one linked-peak comparison and at most one multiply-then-divide for the step " ++
        "plus one per channel for the gain (in i64, no overflow), the gain comp/e in (0, 1]; the attack and release fractions per sample index; no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no " ++
        "real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
